#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb03cc2c-9e09-547d-b74f-c39558b02352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294956} {:answer/id #uuid "17368231-e783-5570-aa08-5bdbf30e2be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294957} {:answer/id #uuid "7763fcc3-fbbf-50a9-8614-a5de03c7d06b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294958} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e54bc3-54c1-5195-97c7-60716c5b6a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294959} {:answer/id #uuid "f26d01e5-939c-591e-896f-2ffc3a8e048f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e54bc3-54c1-5195-97c7-60716c5b6a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294959} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7005e37-c07b-5853-aa37-7ba42e2c2fb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0ab17cf-90f2-5901-a4a1-5dce6cfcb168" :question/math-academy-id "q-164138" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2xy}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1/2,1)} & ={(\\frac{y(1-2x)}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}})|}_{(-1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))}{{(1+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1/2,1)}=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$ Therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6f09894-745c-5512-a3a4-f3906fb13303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348902} {:answer/id #uuid "cff59460-e010-5a83-830f-5102724fe809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348903} {:answer/id #uuid "aadac8fe-099c-5fce-b6ea-f4485731b705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348904} {:answer/id #uuid "f57cf218-7869-5a35-8824-d99a645722e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348905} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc253ea-4ae9-5a7b-8f0e-f1d499dc9c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff59460-e010-5a83-830f-5102724fe809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348903} :answer-field/id #uuid "80d5d792-c634-530d-b38f-581f783df1f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b03dd7a9-474a-5ef8-b096-f3a65f8f342e" :question/math-academy-id "q-89876" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(0,-1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =(y+2x)+2 \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =y+2x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-1)} & ={(y+2x+2)|}_{(0,-1)} \\\\  & =-1+2(0)+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b72b13f1-ae70-5825-9154-5a1314671bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294994} {:answer/id #uuid "3702ba5b-f03c-57e2-8985-181ba039a003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294995} {:answer/id #uuid "444fbe07-d59a-5e73-8271-aeef74c06f6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294996} {:answer/id #uuid "9a175538-7a2f-5a79-9477-8a094acd33ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294997} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec9f59c-e37b-51ed-ad90-eb1e32a3e65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec9f59c-e37b-51ed-ad90-eb1e32a3e65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294998} :answer-field/id #uuid "b30a6b97-037d-55a9-bc98-e278d57d9161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20f93830-129f-5d70-8bb2-53f420e30f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-89880" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}.$ Given that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{(y)^{′}{x}^{2}-y({x}^{2})^{′}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}-2xy)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-2xy}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,2)} & ={\\frac{y(1-2x)}{{x}^{4}}|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-2(1))}{(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c127ac23-d520-5355-98ec-cb418f441597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348909} {:answer/id #uuid "4096e0f9-a0f1-560f-85e3-fa498a58d3dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348910} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6ec02f-cf6f-53dd-83bc-d6975901e784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348911} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d9e98-8878-59e6-b4c3-c1a4bc01e284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348912} {:answer/id #uuid "863cac10-f49c-5934-9897-1c0082fdec93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d9e98-8878-59e6-b4c3-c1a4bc01e284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348912} :answer-field/id #uuid "5efdb9ac-2fb5-58a5-a2b7-e4117779bf78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54ef928c-296a-5b56-8027-31cba563faf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-89884" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}={e}^{y}+x.$ Given that the point $(-1,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(-1,0)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & ={({e}^{y}+x)}^{′} \\\\ ({y}^{′})^{′} & ={({e}^{y})}^{′}+{(x)}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={y}^{′}{e}^{y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute ${({e}^{y})}^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & ={y}^{′}{e}^{y}+1 \\\\ {y}^{″} & =({e}^{y}+x)\\cdot {e}^{y}+1 \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={e}^{2y}+x{e}^{y}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(-1,0)} & ={({e}^{2y}+x{e}^{y}+1)|}_{(-1,0)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(0)}+(-1){e}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f87fb809-b768-536b-a673-297acd18ed8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156827} {:answer/id #uuid "017d2055-3314-53eb-9fdc-3623baa73581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156828} {:answer/id #uuid "435f9c30-e730-553d-8c5f-76f63acfda89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156829} {:answer/id #uuid "49d149b4-349e-5756-b4f5-d1494143593f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156830} {:answer/id #uuid "620ec92d-6935-5875-88b1-3229e3c5bbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "620ec92d-6935-5875-88b1-3229e3c5bbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156831} :answer-field/id #uuid "00673a43-405f-526f-9ba6-48f14156a58e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ad3120d-6b06-5341-b227-fa4c2d83974e" :question/math-academy-id "q-89891" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}]}
